WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.112
The kit's tool has five commands.

2
00:00:02.112 --> 00:00:06.125
Lint applies twelve rules to any
definition, Learn's included.

3
00:00:06.125 --> 00:00:09.871
One of them, L X eleven, adds
up the worst-case retry waits

4
00:00:09.871 --> 00:00:14.200
and fails the definition if they
reach the extension's timeout.

5
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.198
Run runs a definition
against fakes for a cloud,

6
00:00:17.198 --> 00:00:20.350
with downstream failures
and repeat deliveries.

7
00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:24.693
Build writes the Logic App's ARM
resource and the extension's body,

8
00:00:24.693 --> 00:00:27.025
and refuses if lint finds anything.

9
00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:30.688
And two checks compare what you read
back from Graph with the registry.

10
00:00:30.688 --> 00:00:35.560
For authorization, the builder writes the
proof-of-possession policy from Learn's

11
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.930
page, Configure a Logic App for Lifecycle
Workflow use, with shared access

12
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.291
signatures turned off,

13
00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:45.088
as Learn's best practices for
securing the custom extension ask.

14
00:00:45.088 --> 00:00:48.416
Learn documents that policy
only for Azure commercial,

15
00:00:48.416 --> 00:00:52.975
so for a Logic App in Azure Government
the builder refuses rather than guess.

16
00:00:52.975 --> 00:00:56.139
The callback identity needs
no tenant-wide Lifecycle

17
00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:59.163
Workflows permission, which
Learn advises against.

18
00:00:59.163 --> 00:01:04.651
And the workflow check requires the leaver
to block first, which is this workbook's

19
00:01:04.651 --> 00:01:09.480
guidance, in Microsoft's order: disable,
then revoke, then the custom task.
